    "case_id": "731",
    "case_summary": "A 36-year-old female presented with an 18-year history of intermittent dizziness and blurred vision, accompanied by nausea and vomiting. Symptoms would improve after vomiting. In May 2020, the patient developed posterior occipital tingling, which worsened with emotional agitation but would subside spontaneously after a few minutes. By September 2021, symptoms of dizziness and vomiting became more frequent, leading to hospital consultation.\n\nNo specific physical examination findings were reported in the case.\n\n- Imaging Studies:    CT Scan:    - Irregular high-density mass shadow in posterior fossa    - CT value approximately 93HU    - Few bone density shadows at posterior edge        MRI Findings:    - Irregular mixed signals in medial and posterior cerebellum    - Maximum size: 50mm × 41mm × 51mm    - Line-like septum and complex nodules present    - Nodule size: 15mm × 16mm × 13mm    - T1WI: Equal or high signals in cystic part    - T2WI and T2 FLAIR: Low signals    - DWI: Low signals, no diffusion restriction in mural nodules and septum    - SWI: Multiple patchy low and high signals in nodular region        Laboratory Tests:    - Cerebrospinal fluid: AFP (negative), β-HCG (negative)    - Cyst fluid analysis:      * Total protein: 15 g/l      * Albumin: 6.4 g/l      * Globulin: 8.6 g/l      * Total cholesterol: 0.71 mmol/l      * Triglyceride: 0.3 mmol/l      * Melanin: Not detected",
